Section One &middot; &#x05D0;

The general principle relevant to our subject (haklal l&rsquo;inyaneinu) is: one must see to it that one merits to perform the commandment with such great joy (simchah gedolah) that one does not desire any reward of the World to Come &mdash; but rather desires only that Hashem blessed-be-He grant one the merit to perform another commandment as the reward for this commandment. As they said: &ldquo;the reward of a commandment is a commandment&rdquo; (sechar mitzvah mitzvah) etc. And this is the aspect of: &ldquo;On his day you shall give his wages&rdquo; (b&rsquo;yomo siten secharo) etc. (Devarim 24:15). And this is what our Sages of blessed memory said: &ldquo;On Shabbos remember it from the first day of the week&rdquo; (b&rsquo;Shabbos zachrehu me&rsquo;echad b&rsquo;Shabbas) &mdash; that is: the joy and delight of the World to Come, which is the aspect of Shabbos, one should feel during the six days of the work-week, which are the aspect of the deeds of the commandments, through which the worlds of Space, Time, and Soul (olam shanah nefesh) etc. were created. And the essential joy is in the heart etc.

And it is impossible for the heart to rejoice until it strips away the crookedness of the heart (akmimiyas shebalev), so that it has straightness of heart (yashrut leiv) &mdash; and then it merits joy, as it is written: &ldquo;and for the upright of heart, joy&rdquo; (u&rsquo;lyisherai leiv simchah) (Tehillim 97:11). And the crookedness of the heart is stripped away through thunderclaps (re&rsquo;amim) etc. And thunderclaps are the aspect of powerful prayer (tefilah b&rsquo;koach) etc.

But it is impossible to merit the aspect of thunderclaps &mdash; which is powerful prayer that strips away the crookedness of the heart &mdash; except when one clears (mefanin) the mind (mochin) of leaven (chametz): so that one not leaven one&rsquo;s thought (yachmitz es machshavto), heaven forbid, with external wisdoms (chochmos chitzoniyos) and with desires and with stray thoughts (hirhhurim) etc. And one must join the gevuros with the chasadim &mdash; left within right (smola bimina). For from the right side (sistra diyimina) the mind is white as silver (mocha hivara k&rsquo;kaspa) &mdash; for the essential purification of the mind so that it be brilliant as pure silver is from the right side. And this is: &ldquo;and the sea returned toward morning to its original strength&rdquo; etc. (Shemos 14:27). For the essential revelation is through the chasadim &mdash; as it is written: &ldquo;Sit at My right hand&rdquo; (shev limini) etc. (Tehillim 110:1). See there all of this carefully.

Section Two &middot; &#x05D1;

And this is the aspect of Chalah that must be given to the Kohen from the dough &mdash; for one must guard the mind very carefully from leaven (chametz), so that it not leaven one&rsquo;s understanding (da&rsquo;ato), heaven forbid, with evil thoughts as mentioned &mdash; for this is the aspect of chametz, which is the side of death (sitra d&rsquo;mosa). And when one guards against chametz &mdash; that is, from evil thoughts that stupefy (metamtmin) the mind, heaven forbid &mdash; then one merits the aspect of thunderclaps and the crookedness of the heart is stripped away, and one merits joy etc. as mentioned.

And therefore at the beginning of Israel&rsquo;s drawing near (hiskarves) to their Father in Heaven &mdash; that is, on Pesach &mdash; one must be exceedingly careful regarding even a trace of chametz (mashehu chametz). And the essential prohibition of chametz is in bread &mdash; the five grain species (chameshet minei dagan) &mdash; for bread sustains the heart, as our Sages of blessed memory said: pita sa&rsquo;ada d&rsquo;liba &mdash; as it is written (Tehillim 104:15): &ldquo;and bread sustains the heart of man.&rdquo; For by bread man lives, and the essential vitality is wisdom, intellect, and mind &mdash; as it is written (Koheles 7:12): &ldquo;wisdom gives life to those who possess it.&rdquo;

And therefore the essential vitality received through eating &mdash; and principally through bread &mdash; is the mochin and the da&rsquo;as drawn through eating, in the aspect of &ldquo;eat, beloved ones&rdquo; (ichlu rei&rsquo;im) (Shir HaShirim 5:1) &mdash; which are the two companions (trein re&rsquo;in) that are never separated (d&rsquo;la misparshan), the mochin drawn through eating. And this is the essential sustaining of the heart received through eating. For when the mochin are whole, through eating in holiness: one merits that the crookedness of the heart be stripped away, and one merits straightness of heart. And this is the essential vitality and delight of the heart when it is stripped of its crookedness &mdash; and one merits joy, in the aspect of &ldquo;and for the upright of heart, joy.&rdquo;

And this is the aspect of holy eating (achilah dikedushah) &mdash; in the aspect of (Koheles 8:15): &ldquo;there is no good in man except to eat and to drink and to rejoice and to see good&rdquo; etc. For when one merits holy eating &mdash; the aspect of eating matzah &mdash; one merits through such eating joy: the aspect of &ldquo;to eat and to drink and to rejoice.&rdquo; For through matzah one merits thunderclaps, through which the crookedness of the heart is stripped away, through which one merits joy as mentioned.

And therefore Israel &mdash; the holy people &mdash; must have their eating always throughout the year in the aspect of matzah, which is holy eating. As our Sages of blessed memory said: &ldquo;The Torah was given only to those who eat the manna.&rdquo; And manna (man) is the aspect of matzah &mdash; as our Sages of blessed memory said (Kiddushin 38a): the ugah &mdash; that is, the matzah that Israel took out of Egypt &mdash; they tasted in it the taste of manna. For Israel must uphold the Torah, and the essential fulfillment of Torah and commandments in wholeness is the joy of the commandment (simchas hamitzvah) &mdash; as is brought in the writings of the holy Ari of blessed memory, that the essential wholeness is the joy of the commandment. And as it is written (Devarim 28:47): &ldquo;because you did not serve Hashem your G-d with joy and gladness of heart&rdquo; etc. And as is explained in the words of our master of blessed memory (Rabbeinu z&rdquo;l) in the aforementioned Torah and in many places.

And the essential joy of the commandment is when one merits the aforementioned joy &mdash; that one has joy from the commandment itself etc. And this joy is impossible to merit except through matzah &mdash; which is the aspect of manna &mdash; through which one merits thunderclaps, strips away the crookedness of the heart, merits straightness of heart, and then merits the joy of the commandment. And therefore the Torah was given only to those who eat manna &mdash; which is the aspect of matzah, the rectification of the mind in great holiness, through which one specifically merits joy as mentioned &mdash; that is: to fulfill the commandments with joy, which is the essential receiving of the Torah. And this is the aspect of the joy of Shavuos &mdash; as we find that the early righteous ones rejoiced greatly on Shavuos, the receiving of the Torah. For the essential receiving of the Torah is the joy of the commandment &mdash; as it is written (Yeshayahu 35:10): &ldquo;and eternal joy upon their heads&rdquo; &mdash; which was said concerning the two crowns that Israel merited when they said Na&rsquo;aseh v&rsquo;Nishma (Shabbos 88a).

And therefore Israel must always eat in the aspect of matzah, the aspect of manna &mdash; that is: to greatly sanctify their eating, so that through eating they merit holy and pure minds (mochin kedoshim v&rsquo;zakkim). For this is the aspect of matzah &mdash; for we eat actual matzah for the seven days of Pesach, which is a remedy (asvata) through which we sanctify our minds and understanding, which is the essential holiness. And then, having received the remedy for the mind at Pesach, we can afterward eat chametz as well &mdash; as is stated in the Zohar. For through the remedy of matzah eaten at Pesach one sanctifies the mind so greatly that even when eating chametz afterward it is in great holiness &mdash; to the point that one can merit pure minds even through eating chametz all year. For we can transform from chametz to matzah, from ches to heh etc.

And through this one grows strong and brings out the dough from the side of death (sitra d&rsquo;mosa), from the aspect of chametz, and merits that it be in the aspect of matzah, the aspect of thunderclaps &mdash; from which comes the essential growth of bread &mdash; as it is written (Tehillim 104:7,14): &ldquo;from the sound of Your thunder they were startled&hellip; to bring forth bread from the earth.&rdquo; And therefore the essential [moment] of Chalah is when the dough is made &mdash; that is, when water is placed in it, at which point it comes within the scope of leavening (limklal chimutz) &mdash; and then one must sanctify the dough through the Chalah given to the Kohen: through which one grows strong through the aspect of right and elevates from chametz to matzah as mentioned.

Section Four &middot; &#x05D3;

And therefore the custom is to be meticulous to bake bread for Shabbos in order to separate Chalah. And as is written in the Shulchan Aruch &mdash; that even those who eat non-Jewish bread bake bread for Shabbos in order to separate Chalah &mdash; as is written in Orach Chaim, Section 242. And likewise all the Shabbos bread is called chalah &mdash; for the essential separation of Chalah is in order to merit the holiness of Shabbos: which is the joy of the commandment mentioned, where one feels the World to Come &mdash; which is Shabbos &mdash; in the commandment itself. This is the aspect of Shabbos, the aspect of &ldquo;on Shabbos remember it from the first day&rdquo; etc. And this joy one merits through the aspect of matzah, through the aspect of thunderclaps as mentioned &mdash; which one merits through the aspect of right (yamin), which is the aspect of the Chalah given to the Kohen as mentioned. And therefore one must be all the more meticulous to fulfill the commandment of Chalah in the Shabbos bread as mentioned.

And this is the aspect of Shabbos eating, which is a great commandment &mdash; for Shabbos eating is holy eating, the aspect of manna &mdash; as is written in the Shulchan Aruch: that at Shabbos eating one makes a commemoration of the manna that was as if placed in a box &mdash; which is the aspect of the two matzos (shetei hamappos) between which the Shabbos bread is placed etc. For on Shabbos &mdash; which is the aspect of the World to Come felt in this world when one merits to receive Shabbos properly &mdash; it is a great commandment to eat. For then eating is in holiness, the aspect of manna, the aspect of matzah, in the aspect of &ldquo;so that you may be nourished and delight yourselves&rdquo; &mdash; the aspect of oneg Shabbos. As our master of blessed memory explained in another place (Section 57). And through such eating one merits the joy of the commandment &mdash; which is the essential oneg Shabbos: feeling the delight and joy of the World to Come, which is the aspect of Shabbos, in this world as mentioned. And this is &ldquo;to eat and to drink and to rejoice&rdquo; &mdash; which was said concerning Shabboses and Festivals, as is brought in the Midrash.

Section Five &middot; &#x05D4;

And this is the aspect of the double bread (lechem mishneh) eaten on Shabbos &mdash; this is the aspect of Mishneh Torah [the doubled Torah] &mdash; which is the aspect of &ldquo;therefore in their land they shall inherit double&rdquo; (lachein b&rsquo;artzam mishneh yirashu) (Yeshayahu 61:7). Which is the aspect of &ldquo;she does not fear for her house from snow, for all her household is clothed in crimson&rdquo; (lo sira l&rsquo;veisah mishaleg ki kol beisah lavush shanim) (Mishlei 31:21). And our Sages of blessed memory expounded: lavush shnayim [clothed in two] &mdash; that the Torah doubles its words (kofellet devarehah) in all commandments. As it is written: &ldquo;return, you shall return&rdquo; (hashev tashiv), &ldquo;give generously, you shall give&rdquo; (ha&rsquo;aneik ta&rsquo;anik), &ldquo;give, you shall give&rdquo; (naton titen), &ldquo;open, you shall open&rdquo; (pasoach tiftach) etc. &mdash; that is: the Torah doubles its words in most commandments, to teach that the essential fulfillment of commandments should be for the sake of doing another commandment as the reward for this commandment &mdash; as they said: &ldquo;the reward of a commandment is a commandment.&rdquo; And this is the essential wholeness of fulfilling commandments with joy as mentioned.
